      "a": "Here,\nThe given temperature, $T=~27{}^\\circ C$\u00a0 can be written in Kelvin as $$27+273=300\\text{ }K$$.\nOuter diameter of the steel shaft at T,\u00a0 ${{d}_{1}}=8.70cm$\nDiameter of the central hole in the wheel at T,\u00a0 ${{d}_{2}}=8.69cm$\nCoefficient of linear expansion of steel, $:{{\\alpha }_{steel}}=1.20\\times {{10}^{-5}}{{K}^{-1}}$\nAfter the shaft is cooled using \u2018dry ice\u2019, its temperature becomes ${{T}_{1}}$.\nThe wheel will slip on the shaft, if the change in diameter, $$\\Delta d=8.698.70=0.01$$cm\nTemperature ${{T}_{1}}$ , can be calculated from the relation:\n$$\\Delta d={{d}_{1}}{{\\alpha }_{steel}}\\left( {{T}_{1}}-T \\right)$$\n$$\\Rightarrow \\Delta d=8.70\\times 1.20\\times {{10}^{-5}}\\left( {{T}_{1}}-300 \\right)$$\n$\\Rightarrow \\left( {{T}_{1}}-300 \\right)=95.78$\n$\\Rightarrow {{T}_{1}}=204.21K$\nOr ${{T}_{1}}=204.21-273.16=-68.95{}^\\circ C$\nClearly, the wheel will slip on the shaft when the temperature of the shaft is approximately $-69{}^\\circ C.$"
